# Coverage floor. May only RISE; the gate's `fix` half refuses to record a
# drop (SPEC.md V27). Raised in five rounds from 0.0 at the scaffold:
#
#   63.68  the config loader arrived with tests
#   85.99  splitter and corpus reader
#   91.99  scan pipeline tested against a real directory
#   93.93  dispatch moved out of main.rs, exit codes made assertable
#   95.89  error paths, config discovery, classifier fallbacks
#   96.64  the command path end to end
#   97.07  binary success path, ordered-marker rejection
#
# PRACTICAL CEILING, measured rather than guessed. The ~51 lines still
# uncovered are, in order of size:
#
#   ~27  `unreachable!`/`panic!` arms inside TEST fixtures. They execute
#        only when a fixture is itself broken, so covering them means
#        deliberately breaking one.
#     4  `fn main` -- a binary entry point cannot be called by a unit test.
#     3  `dispatch`, the one-line shim main calls. Covering it would assert
#        nothing: `ExitCode` implements no comparison, which is why
#        `perform` returns a u8 instead.
#     ~7  walk-error construction and its Display. Reachable only with a
#        directory that exists but cannot be read, which needs a chmod that
#        does nothing when tests run as root -- a test that passes silently
#        in one environment is worse than an uncovered line.
#     ~4  `current_dir()` failing, `GlobSet::build()` failing: error paths
#        with no portable way to provoke them.
#
# So ~97-98% is the real ceiling for this shape of crate, and chasing 100
# would mean testing the test harness. Raise this when new code lands with
# tests, not by finding ways to execute the lines above.

